   "verdict": "MECHANISM SURVIVES, BYTE-IDENTITY DOES NOT, and the ablated arm's own metric contribution is sample-dependent noise. At turn grain the guard-ablated top-5 is NO LONGER byte-identical to OFF (3/20 questions differ: S1 4f54b7c9, S2 3c1045c8 and S2 60036106), unlike the chunk-grain receipt (10/10 identical). But every id that ENTERS the top-5 is a BASE item already present in OFF's ranked pool (2/2 membership changes; the third question is a pure order change) - ZERO injected items enter hits[:5] without the guard. So the mechanism claim in the previous receipt (the injected items are the trigger, not the entering items) still holds, while the claim as stated there - that the two arms are indistinguishable - does not. The practical consequence is a measurement-validity one: inj_only moves recall_all@5 by +0.1 on S1 and -0.1 on S2 (net 0.0 pooled), so a single-sample reading would credit the guard with a +/-0.1 swing that is not there."
